179:   /** Perform the Critic's analysis of the design. Subclasses should test
180:    *  the given Object to make sure that it is the type of
181:    *  object that is expected.  Each object in the design registers its
182:    *  own critics with the run-time system. The dm parameter is bound
183:    *  to each design object that registered this critic, one per
184:    *  call. Returning true means that feedback should be delivered to
185:    *  the Designer. By convention, subclasses should return their
186:    *  superclass predicate method if their own predicate would
187:    *  return false. */
188:   public boolean predicate(Object dm, Designer dsgr) {
189:     return false;
190:   }
191: 
192:   /** Return true iff the given ToDoItem is still valid and should be
193:    *  kept in the given designers ToDoList. Critics that are not
194:    *  enabled should always return false so that their ToDoItems will
195:    *  be removed. Subclasses of Critic that supply multiple offenders
196:    *  should always override this method. <p>
197:    *
198:    *  By default this method basically asks the critic to again
199:    *  critique the offending Object and then it checks if the
200:    *  resulting ToDoItem is the same as the one already posted. This is
201:    *  simple and it works fine for light-weight critics. Critics that
202:    *  expend a lot of computational effort in making feedback that can
203:    *  be easily check to see if it still holds, should override this
204:    *  method. <p>
205:    *
206:    *  Needs-More-Work: Maybe ToDoItem should carry some data to make
207:    *  this method more efficient. */
208:   public boolean stillValid(ToDoItem i, Designer dsgr) {
209:     if (!isActive()) return false;
210:     if (i.getOffenders().size() != 1) return true;
211:     if (predicate(i.getOffenders().firstElement(), dsgr)) {
212:       ToDoItem item = toDoItem(i.getOffenders().firstElement(), dsgr);
213:       return (item.equals(i));
214:     }
215:     return false;
216:   }
